{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers","id":807844,"title":"Hunter 2E Open Dev Follow-Up: Hunters and Slashers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-01-13T07:00:40-08:00","id":807844,"title":"Hunter 2E Open Dev Follow-Up: Hunters and Slashers","content":"As a follow-up to my Open Dev post, I wanted to mention that I can confirm that I am seeking to clarify definitions for both Hunters and Slashers, and that may stretch to clarifying what monsters are as well. This is similar to clarifying tiered play, too, and I want to emphasis that that is not the same as &quot;fixing&quot; issues, because Hunter 1E is a great game as is. These clarifications will be presented in the same toolkit format that has been used in the past, both semantically and mechanically, but I feel as developer these ideas are something to explore in the brainstorming phase. I tend to be very thorough but dense in my development/writing style, and this often translates into the addition of a sidebar or a small section in some cases. In other instances, due to the rules changes from 1E to 2E, more effort may be required from a systems perspective. I'm also the type of dev that doesn't differentiate between &quot;fluff&quot; and &quot;crunch&quot;; I think of the rules as the engine of a game, and the setting as the vehicle. Thus, I feel that the rules should absolutely support the basic concepts of the game, regardless, and I want to get the most out of 2E to enhance gameplay.<br /> <br /> One of the things I've noticed from the beginning is that your (e.g. the fans) iconic definition of a Hunter is so varied, it's also bleeding over into how people think of Slashers in general. Are Slashers a type of Hunter? My feeling on this is that they are not, but this is why I need to take a closer look at these basic definitions. To me, this means that there's confusion occurring at some point, and those questions may translate to a frustration with how the 2E systems are presented or a lack of understanding as to what the Vigil is, what a slasher is, and perhaps what a monster is as well.<br /> <br /> If you'd like to help shape these definitions that will be supported by the 2E rules, I think the forums is a good place to discuss what the iconic, human &quot;Hunter&quot; who has no access to the supernatural is. Then, take that same Hunter, and tell me what the heroic and villainous versions are. I'm also interested in how the definitions shift between a Hunter who has access to the supernatural, and one who does not.<br /> <br /> I'd like to reinforce that nothing is set in stone at this point, and I haven't made any clear decisions yet. These discussions are hugely important--especially prior to the outline phase. Looking forward to seeing your thoughts on this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1870,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1870-mlvalentine","user":"mlvalentine","date":".  Forgot my last thought. I blame a lack of coffee. 01-13-2016, 10:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1870-mlvalentine","name":"mlvalentine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1870_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T07:10:39-08:00","id":807848,"title":"","content":"Hm, personally I've always seen a strong difference between the two. A hunter being a human who takes up weapons against the entities in the world of darkness that prey on humanity, while a slasher is a serial killer whose actions have somehow imbued them with supernatural powers.<br /> <br /> Now I can see a hunter who goes insane thanks to the stress of the vigil becoming a Slasher, I don't personally want the mechanics to make this a normal event. To me, every serial killer should not be a slasher, a slasher should still be a rare and dangerous opponent for hunters to hunt.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5439-gullinbursti","name":"Gullinbursti","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5439_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"291"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T07:14:02-08:00","id":807851,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gullinbursti</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807848#post807848\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hm, personally I've always seen a strong difference between the two. A hunter being a human who takes up weapons against the entities in the world of darkness that prey on humanity, while a slasher is a serial killer whose actions have somehow imbued them with supernatural powers.<br /> <br /> Now I can see a hunter who goes insane thanks to the stress of the vigil becoming a Slasher, I don't personally want the mechanics to make this a normal event. To me, every serial killer should not be a slasher, a slasher should still be a rare and dangerous opponent for hunters to hunt.</div> </div> </div> </div>It'd be more common in the context of the Slasher Chronicle, but regardless I see: serial killer, Slasher, and Hunter as being three separate but distinct entities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1870-mlvalentine","name":"mlvalentine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1870_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T07:25:05-08:00","id":807861,"title":"","content":"For me the distinction is always the &quot;Buffy factor&quot; and I've introduced houserules and hacks to make that more evident in my games. The question is less &quot;what's the difference between a Hunter and the monsters she hunts?&quot; and more one of &quot;what's the difference between a Hunter and ordinary mortals?&quot; Using the Buffy example, Buffy would be a Hunter but the rest of the &quot;scooby gang&quot; are just ordinary mortals swept up in her hunt. Slashers are Hunters gone bad (at least that's my approach) -- think Faith.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1630-jacob","name":"Jacob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1630_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T07:26:25-08:00","id":807864,"title":"","content":"Hm, well, to me, I guess the definitions would be something along these lines having played HtV,<br /> <br /> Hunter - A human who has taken up weapons (be they mortal or supernatural) against the horrors that prey upon humanity in the world of darkness.<br /> <br /> Monster - An entity that exists in the World of Darkness that preys upon humanity that is supernatural in nature.<br /> <br /> Slasher - A mortal changed by the World of Darkness who feeds upon murder.<br /> <br /> I have no problem with any of these definitions being shifted about or changed as a second edition comes along though. In fact, I was already thinking that something tying Slashers into the mechanisms of the God Machine would be rather interesting.<br /> <br /> Oh, the one thing I would change from the open dev document yesterday though, I'd use something other than Cabal as the group name for Slashers. Mainly because my player group and I would tend to visualize a group of mages before slashers with that name <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5439-gullinbursti","name":"Gullinbursti","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5439_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"291"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T08:20:13-08:00","id":807903,"title":"","content":"Two things I'd like to see being discussed in 2ED are: at which point does a mundane serial killer become a Slasher, and do Hunters gain some kind of pseudo-supernatural benefit by taking up the Vigil?<br /> <br /> I asked the second question because Hunters can do things that regular people just can't, like Risking Willpower and earning Practical Experiences. Some people (myself included) like to make Conviction from the Mirrors supplement the &quot;Powerstat&quot; of Hunters, since it goes very well with Risking Willpower and it's thematically fitting. But is there something more at play than the power of the human spirit? I even read a post once where someone said that Hunters in their games can't acquire supernatural templates: if drained of blood and fed Vitae, they do not become vampires, and so on. I thought that was an interesting house rule and a possible plot hook.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":4443,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","user":"GibberingEloquence","date":". 01-13-2016, 11:22 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T08:38:01-08:00","id":807917,"title":"","content":"Mine definitions of core terms to clarify what it is:<br /> <b>\u200b<br /> Hunter</b> - human (mortal) that are <b><i>obsessed with <u>confronting</u></i></b> <b><i>a monsters</i></b>, as so called Vigil ( and more plainly &quot;hunting&quot; ). They <b>do not need</b> weapons or combat to this - <i>Null Mysteriis</i> and <i>Network Zero</i> are still hunters, even if they typically do not kill any monsters. Still, most hunters use weapons to survive the confrontations.<br /> <br /> <b>Monster</b> - entity that prey on humanity, literally or metaphorically. <i>Most</i> monsters are supernatural, but <b>not all</b> supernatural are monsters. There are some examples of &quot;human monsters&quot; - people so dangerous for population they need to be put to rest. If monster looks and act like human, hunting on him is much more difficult (many times risking Integrity).<br /> <br /> <b>Serial Killer </b>- human who kills other people in large numbers. Many can become Slashers. Other could still be hunters, if they dedicate themselves to kill &quot;human monsters&quot;. Man killing more than two terrorist is, technically, a serial killer.<br /> <br /> <b>Slasher</b> - human who become obsessed with killing so much, it's open him to some dark forces of Chronicle of Darkness universe. He has <b><i>urge</i></b> to kill people and he cannot stop it. He can rationalize himself it, but deep under his consciousness there is awoken <i>instinct</i> to kill others. He can have smaller or larger Dread Powers as Undertakings. Most dedicated serial killers become Slashers. Hunters can become Slashers often - as hunters obsession with confronting other beings can lend to obsession in to killing other brings - and from them to killing humans. Slashers can sate their hunger for killing preying on monsters ( like <i><b>Dexter</b></i> in titular show is doing ) but in the long term they need human victims.<br /> <br /> Heroic and Villainous versions of Hunters:<br /> <br /> <b>Heroic Hunter</b> - Someone that in his obsession to confront monsters is doing this in the name of saving others lives. They do what they need to <b><i>save</i></b> people. Like Sam and Dean in last <b>Supernatural</b> season - <i>&quot;Saving people <b>does not</b> mean killing monsters&quot;</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>Villainous Hunter</b> - Someone who is so obsessed with confronting monsters, he will use other people to get to kill monster. Those do not save people - they sacrifice people to let them kill the monster they are all from the start.<br /> <br /> <b>Slashers can come from Villainous Hunters as they make shift from &quot;sacrifice people to kill monster&quot; in to &quot;kill people&quot; themselves.</b><br /> <br /> Having or not Endowments does not have anything to being Heroic, Villainous or Slasher Hunter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T08:38:52-08:00","id":807918,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GottaGoFeast</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807903#post807903\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Two things I'd like to see being discussed in 2ED are: at which point does a mundane serial killer become a Slasher, and do Hunters gain some kind of pseudo-supernatural benefit by taking up the Vigil?<br /> <br /> I asked the second question because Hunters can do things that regular people just can't, like Risking Willpower and earning Practical Experiences. Some people (myself included) like to make Conviction from the Mirrors supplement the &amp;quot;Powerstat&amp;quot; of Hunters, since it goes very well with Risking Willpower and it's thematically fitting. But is there something more at play than the power of the human spirit? I even read a post once where someone said that Hunters in their games can't acquire supernatural templates: if drained of blood and fed Vitae, they do not become vampires, and so on. I thought that was an interesting house rule and a possible plot hook.</div> </div> </div> </div>Both good questions. I can totally see someone like Habnnibal, Frank, or the Boston Ripper making the jump from a mundane serial killer to a Slasher just as easily as a Hunter who gets lost in the Vigil.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T08:40:24-08:00","id":807920,"title":"","content":"DaveB has mentioned in the past that humans in CofD have a tendency to shy away from the supernatural. That is why one of the five Breaking Point questions in the CofD Rulebook is \u201cwhat [supernatural encounter] have you forgotten?\u201d \u2014 the premise behind that question is that virtually everyone in the setting has had an encounter with the supernatural at one point or another, and nearly all of them have put that encounter out of their mind, willfully forgetting what they saw. The protagonists of the CofD core book are among these \u201cordinary people\u201d, but have recently had (or are about to have) an encounter with the darkness that they cannot ignore. That's the framework of the \u201cdefault\u201d Chronicle of Darkness: ordinary people dealing as best they can with the horrors and nightmares that lurk in the shadows, despite their natural inclination to hide from it and pretend it doesn't exist. <br /> <br /> By contrast, a hunter is a human who willfully confronts the monsters on a regular basis. Whatever it is that causes ordinary people to ignore the monsters has no hold on the hunter; instead, he or she does the exact opposite and <i>seeks out</i> the supernatural. That's what the Vigil is: a reversal of humanity's normal instincts concerning the CofD's mysteries. <br /> <br /> That's the conceptual distinction between a \u201cblue book mortal\u201d and a hunter, as I see it. The mechanical distinction is almost entirely embodied in Practical Experience and its applications (in 1e, Risking Willpower is the only \u201ctemplate ability\u201d that hunters have that isn't purchased with Practical Experience): as I like to think of it, Practical Experience is what a hunter learns from an encounter that a non-hunter would overlook or forget.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T08:51:15-08:00","id":807926,"title":"","content":"Hunters: humans that have the willpower and conviction to defend others against the dark forces that prey on humanity.<br /> <br /> Serial killer: a human that preys on humanity typically those who are most at risk and has committed more than 3 killings.<br /> <br /> Slasher: a broken human that exists only to kill.<br /> In an upcoming chronicle I intend to use the detective from the 13th precinct source book as a budding slasher. In his zeal to deny the supernatural he has killed innocent people by framing them for murder.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/293-lncbill","name":"lncbill","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar293_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T08:57:21-08:00","id":807929,"title":"","content":"Slashers are serial killers who <i>must</i> kill. It's not just that they enjoy it, it's what they are. It's what they have become.<br /> <br /> Not all Hunters kill, and not all who kill enjoy it, or consider it to be what they are. Their existence goes beyond the act of killing.<br /> <br /> A Hunter confronts the darkness. A Slasher's existence is defined by murder.<br /> <br /> Honestly, if it wasn't for Hunter going with &quot;The Slasher Chronicles&quot;, I wouldn't even associate Hunters and Slashers together. To me, Slashers are the universal monster.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T09:18:31-08:00","id":807949,"title":"","content":"I like the concept of Slasher cabals, however I also agree that Slashers in general do not play well with others. I would like options for a cult like organization that actively cultivated Slashers by protecting serial killers and recruiting borderline Hunters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/293-lncbill","name":"lncbill","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar293_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T09:32:54-08:00","id":807956,"title":"","content":"\u2022 serial killer: someone who has no problem killing. <br /> \u2022 ripper: a serial killer who is <i>driven</i> to kill. <br /> \u2022 scourge: a ripper who has literally become inhuman. <br /> <br /> In 1e, \u201cslasher\u201d meant \u201cripper or scourge\u201d, and furthermore involved an Undertaking that put the slasher a cut above an ordinary mortal (if a ripper) or turned him into an inhuman killer (if a scourge). In some ways, they felt a bit like <b>Hunter: the Reckoning</b>'s Imbued, in that even a ripper was empowered. In 2e, I'm hoping that the definition of \u201cslasher\u201d gets expanded to include serial killers, leading to something like a three-tier setup, with killers being the \u201cEntry-level Slashers\u201d who don't have any extraordinary or supernatural abilities. This makes it easier to slide over from Hunter to Slasher, since the distinction between hunter and killer is so easy to blur.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T09:38:36-08:00","id":807961,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807920#post807920\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">By contrast, a hunter is a human who willfully confronts the monsters on a regular basis. Whatever it is that causes ordinary people to ignore the monsters has no hold on the hunter; instead, he or she does the exact opposite and <i>seeks out</i> the supernatural. That's what the Vigil is: a reversal of humanity's normal instincts concerning the CofD's mysteries.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">That's the conceptual distinction between a \u201cblue book mortal\u201d and a hunter, as I see it. The mechanical distinction is almost entirely embodied in Practical Experience and its applications (in 1e, Risking Willpower is the only \u201ctemplate ability\u201d that hunters have that isn't purchased with Practical Experience): as I like to think of it, Practical Experience is what a hunter learns from an encounter that a non-hunter would overlook or forget.</span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I completely agree with this. In fact in my own GMC / Mortal Remains Hunter the Vigil game I've replaced the question, &quot;What have you forgotten?&quot; with &quot;What can't you forget?&quot; Which served to give players a definite motivation to hunt and me ideas about what sort of monsters they want to see. For example one PC survived a Slasher attack, when the Cell encountered a Slasher it put his character in the spotlight. Hunters are human - they're just incredibly committed they can't turn back.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Slashers are people who have allowed evil to bloom like a cancer until it empowers and compels them to murder. I think its worthwhile to model them after both serial killers and the 'movie slashers', but also to not make it impossibly easy to spot them or tell the difference between say a regular serial killer and a Slasher. They are an irredeemable end to the Hunt, but I don't think they should be the sole end point for a Hunter who has lost his way. Consider Rotschreck from Watchmen or Dexter, they're both killers but they live by a strict code they still hunt 'monsters' not 'innocents'. These monstrous hunters are a staple of the modern monster hunter or superhero genre that I think trying to combine with Slashers would be a missed opportunity. Monstrous, fanatical, supernaturally corrupted Hunters are still Hunters - they could be redeemed.</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1783,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1783-spanishinquisition","user":"SpanishInquisition","date":". 01-14-2016, 12:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1783-spanishinquisition","name":"SpanishInquisition","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1783_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T09:45:42-08:00","id":807966,"title":"","content":"Here's my definitions: <hr /><br /> <b>Hunter: </b>A mortal human or minor template (Thamaturge, Ghoul, Wolfblood - they may not have a Supernatural Advantage) who has overcome the urge to pretend monsters do not exist that is common to humans in the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> <b>Heroic Hunter examples</b><ul><li>Take up arms to defend mortals from supernatural predation.</li> <li>Acquire supernatural power for virtuous reasons. Such as healing magic.</li> <li>Study the supernatural knowledge for virtuous reasons. Such as a priest communing with the gods so that they can be a better spiritual leader.</li> <li>Live normal lives but advise the people around them. E.G. Comforting a friend who was bitten by a vampire. (rarely PC material)</li> </ul><br /> <b>Neutral Hunter examples</b><ul><li>Acquire supernatural power for personal gain. E.G. using healing magic for the money.</li> <li>Acquire supernatural knowledge for personal gain. E.G. A Null Mysteriis who's just in it for curiosity.</li> <li>Adopt an occult lifestyle simply because it works for you. E.G. joining the Silver Ladder because you think working magic and the doctrine combined make a compelling argument.</li> <li>Live a perfectly normal life, don't deny the supernatural exists, but don't pay attention to it. (rarely PC material)</li> <li>Grab some popcorn, watch the latest output from Network zero. (rarely PC material)</li> </ul><br /> <b>Villainous Hunter examples</b><ul><li>Acquire supernatural power for personal gain and without ethical restraint. E.G. Selling your firstborn son to a demon</li> <li>Take up arms to defend mortals from supernatural predation and no ethical restraints. E.G. Blowing up an orphanage with one monster inside.</li> </ul><br /> <br /> <b>Monster: </b>Any supernatural creature that has at least one dot in a Power Trait (Wyrd, Primal Urge, Gnosis, Rank, etc). Supernatural creatures that are notably inhuman may qualify even without a power trait.<br /> <br /> <b>Slasher: </b>A specific kind of antagonist, designed to mechanically portray the villains of the Slasher Movie genera. Note that I would consider Rippers to be probably not monsters by the above definition but Scourges to be monsters; that said I do give Scourges a Power Trait.<br /> <br /> As a guideline for what I mean by &quot;mechanically portray the villains of the Slasher Movie genera&quot; I consider the following four traits key to Slashers.<br /> <br /> 1. They're a serial killer who primarally targets regular mortals.<br /> <br /> 2. They fall on a power level ranging from non-supernatural but inhumanly skilled (think Jigsaw) to low powered supernatural: Freddy Kruger is undeniably using supernatural abilities but he's only got a few tricks and I would say that a Mage or Changeling could easily outdo him at dream manipulation.<br /> <br /> 3. They have an exceptionally strong sense of theme that they're psychologically or supernaturally compelled to follow. Iconic outfits, a persistent modus operandi, favoured weapons. Etc<br /> <br /> 4. A Slasher's psychological issues form the core of their charachter and are the source of their borderline-supernatural or outright-supernatural abilities.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":134,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","user":"The Kings Raven","date":". 01-13-2016, 12:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T09:57:36-08:00","id":807973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mlvalentine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers#post807844\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you'd like to help shape these definitions that will be supported by the 2E rules, I think the forums is a good place to discuss what the iconic, human &quot;Hunter&quot; who has no access to the supernatural is. Then, take that same Hunter, and tell me what the heroic and villainous versions are. I'm also interested in how the definitions shift between a Hunter who has access to the supernatural, and one who does not.<br /> <br /> I'd like to reinforce that nothing is set in stone at this point, and I haven't made any clear decisions yet. These discussions are hugely important--especially prior to the outline phase. Looking forward to seeing your thoughts on this.</div> </div> </div> </div>for me, the most iconic &quot;Hunter&quot; is the best that humanity has to offer- how people draw strength from their hope and despair in order to challenge creatures who are more powerful than them in more than one way: beings who can torn them to shreds with bare hands, beings who can steal their souls with a whisper, beings who has control over the greatest individuals in society and enough resources to buy a nation. Hunters know all of that and they steal dare to challenge those enemies against all odds- and they actually succeed! They use splinters of information gathered from old books and not very trusty forums, collect some kitchen knives, baseball bats and maybe the gun of their parents and gather their faith and willpower to the endless fight against the monsters. Risking Willpower, Practical Experience and the Code all enhance that feeling, IMO.<br /> <br /> As an hero, that hunter would relay on her virtues and higher nature in order to hold on- she has faith in humanity, she believes her fight is meaningful, she is willing to risk her life for the greater good. She doesn't do it for vengeance or hate- she does it simply because it is the right thing to do. As a villain, she in a nihilist- she hunts because she fears, she hunts because she hates, she hunts because she's hurt. Maybe she doesn't hunt to protect others as much as she tries to protect herself, willing to sacrifice others for her own safety. Maybe she just seeks after vengeance, and she is prepared to do what ever it takes just to kill that specific monster. And then that one. And that one. Maybe she just does it because it is fun- she likes the rush, and doesn't care if people get killed in the way- or even if she dies by herself. All of those are forms of nihilism and fear, unlike those who seek for a brighter future- but at any case, she is still an Hunter, not a Slasher. She would fall into Slasherhood only when she would leave behind both her fear and hope in order to become.. something else, something which is no longer quite human as she once was. Even a villainous hunter may be able to light up a candle, but when you turn into a Slasher, there are only shadows around you.<br /> <br /> Now, let's say that our hunter survive her first few stumbles against the supernatural,and she learns a thing or two about the monsters. She joins (or even forms) a Compact. Now she has some more resources, more friends, more support. In her own turf, she turns into a power one needs to consider- not something too big, not enough to challenge a Covenant or a Lodge, but they are able to present a constant Vigil against random, lone monsters (or even a group of those!). She knows more about what hides in the shadows and how to deal with them- she no longer tries to shoot a werewolf with an iron bullet or thinks she is protected from a vampire just because she didn't invited it in. She can call for help when things goes bad and she has friends she can share with her experiences. She sacrifice some of her freedom, of course, but the chances are that the compact shares most of how she views her hunt. Devoted Christians would join the Long Night, killing monsters with mercy. Scientific researchers would cast their lot among the Null Mysteriis. Hedonists who just want to have fun would buy an Ashwood Abbey License, and so on and so on. An heroic hunter would find comfort in her new place in the world and devout herself to their ideals. She would fear the monsters less. She feel comfort from her friends and partners in the hunt.<br /> <br /> A villainous hunter, however, would have a different view on the world- the more she knows, the more is afraid. Believing that there are vampires in the government is different than actually knowing it. She doesn't see her cell mates as friends as much as they are tools- people die every day on the Vigil, it is better not to get attached. It is ok to send a cell or two to their death, because there are always new hunters in the compact and as long as the beast is dead everyone (she!) is safe. She won't rest as long as there are monsters in the world, no matter how many lives are needed to be sacrificed for that goal. And yet, she is still not a Slasher- only because she directs her efforts toward monsters. She is still sane enough to distinguish between human and inhuman, and through that she still clings to her own humanity. There is a difference between accepting death as an eventual outcome and actively pursuing it. After all, an heroic hunter is as much as in danger for falling as a villainous one- adhering into your Virtues may cause you to put people out of their misery, judging the guilty on your own accord or waging an holy war against anyone who is in touch with a monster- up to the point of seeing other people as monsters.<br /> <br /> After few years in the hunt and showing a surprising ability to survive, out Hunter has managed to join the top of the top, the best among the best- a Conspiracy. Either by birth or skill, she is now a part of a great organization, one of the movers and shakers of the occult world. If a lone hunter is the best that humanity has to offer, a Conspiracy is made by taking all of those small candles and turn them into a lighthouse, guiding and protecting thousands of souls from the monsters. She has enough resources to plan raids and operations against the things in the dark, and she sometimes laugh on the times she believed quartz would actually banish spirits away. For her, hunting monsters is not just a way of life- it is a job, something she is expected to do. She knows how monsters act, what types of monsters are there and how you can kill them, and sometimes she even knows things the monsters themselves are not aware of. Alone, she is weak in powerless- but supported by her organization, she can become something that monsters fear. And not only that, now she even has access to Endowments- supernatural or high mundane abilities which can become the wild card which changes the outcome of battle. Joining a Conspiracy also supports the idea of using whatever you got to fight the darkness mechanically- hunters benefit from their Status, Endowments are just a special set of Merits. The supernatural is a part of her, and she is using it to hunt it down.<br /> <br /> For an heroic character, it may not be easy to adapt to her new situation- Conspiracies are not the place of idealists. There is a chain of command, a way to do things. She is expected to do certain things and don't ask certain questions. She may need to make compromises, unless she follows their point of view with zeal- and even then she is required to restrain herself. She may even feel a bit of hypocrite in case of using Endowments, and avoid using them. She may try to rise in her organization in order to change it, make it into a better one, or she may adapt to it, agreeing that the way they do things is right. She already gets used to monsters, and may start to forget all of early fears and hopes- although she sometimes asks herself: is that the best humanity is capable of? This is truly what I've dreamed about when I've started? Those who stick to their hope may actually make a difference. Those who fall to despair usually vanish one day without trace, only to be found years later, holding a knife and wearing a mask.<br /> <br /> A villainous character may not be much better. Being a part of a conspiracy should be everything she ever wanted to- she is finally protected, she can easily kill her enemy and if she rise in rank she is able to command others to die in the name of her cause. Maybe she agrees with the organization's point of view and fully adhere to it for it makes her feel comfort. Maybe she is just using it as a tool, like she always did. Anyway, now she is a part of a big Conspiracy- yet she still doesn't feel safe yet. She looks around her and doesn't see friends- she sees people who wants something from her, who expect her to follow their orders without question. She sees secrets in every corner, and people who are not willing to talk about them. She sees Endowments and think to herself from where those powers come from and whether her organization is actually working with monsters- or worse, being the power behind the monsters. Some keep those fears to themselves. Others investigate their own organization. Few find answers which they don't like, finally losing hope on mankind. Some breaks, something cracks, the candle falls on the ground- and here comes the chopper!<br /> <br /> In sort, an Hunter is always an Hunter, whether she is an hero or a villain. Knowing about the supernatural may bring her peace, and it may drive her to madness. Not anyone can be an Hunter- only the best that humanity has to offer, and as we all know, it is not a lot.<br /> <br /> (wow- that was looooooong! I really hope you'll find that useful :P and P.S- I really enjoy your blog posts and I like the way you see the interactions between crunch and fluff. It is easy to see you love the game, and I can't wait to see how Hunter 2E would turn out)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T10:00:05-08:00","id":807976,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807956#post807956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u2022 serial killer: someone who has no problem killing.<br /> \u2022 ripper: a serial killer who is <i>driven</i> to kill.<br /> \u2022 scourge: a ripper who has literally become inhuman.<br /> <br /> In 1e, \u201cslasher\u201d meant \u201cripper or scourge\u201d, and furthermore involved an Undertaking that put the slasher a cut above an ordinary mortal (if a ripper) or turned him into an inhuman killer (if a scourge). In some ways, they felt a bit like <b>Hunter: the Reckoning</b>'s Imbued, in that even a ripper was empowered. In 2e, I'm hoping that the definition of \u201cslasher\u201d gets expanded to include serial killers, leading to something like a three-tier setup, with killers being the \u201cEntry-level Slashers\u201d who don't have any extraordinary or supernatural abilities. This makes it easier to slide over from Hunter to Slasher, since the distinction between hunter and killer is so easy to blur.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm going to ask you a ridiculously challenging question then. With what you posted in mind: What's the difference between a serial killer and a <i>hunter</i>?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1870-mlvalentine","name":"mlvalentine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1870_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T10:01:35-08:00","id":807978,"title":"","content":"<b>Heroic Hunter</b>: Head of an information network who keeps Hunters informed about notable supernatural happenings. Acts as a middleman between Cells, to help them maintain their anonymity. Earnings go towards the Vigil and related matters.<br /> <br /> <b>Selfish Hunter</b>: As above, only with a mercenary approach. Help is usually refused without payment - be it money, occasional shady service or stranger things. Earnings may, or may not go towards the Vigil. Uses leverage against their competition, to preserve their niche.<br /> <br /> <b>Corrupt Hunter</b>: The Hunter is now willing to play Cells against each other, just to advance their interests. Uses blackmail and coercion, hires criminal leg-breakers (possibly through different identities). May sell his competitors out to their enemies, mortal authorities and even Monsters. Likely benefits from a criminal enterprise.<br /> <br /> <b>Extremist Hunter</b>: Uses the tactics of a Corrupt Hunter to advance their vision of the Vigil. Makes hard choices that likely don't have to be made. Treats Hunters with different beliefs as enemies. May, or may not be open about their plans.<br /> <br /> <b><i>Compromised Hunter:</i></b> May build upon any of the previous examples. For whatever reason, their activities benefit a supernatural entity (or society). Keeps it informed about going-ons in the Hunter community. Distracts Hunters from targets that matter or uses them as unwitting pawns.<br /> <br /> <b><i>Burned-out Hunter:</i></b> Likewise, may build upon any of the previous examples. Assuming they don't just cease the Vigil, they become unpredictable in the eyes of other Hunters. The quality of their services likely drops. They may wake Hunters during the night with rant-filled phone calls, or show up on TV demolishing the local Police station. With all the information at their disposal they become a massive liability to the Hunter community.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>~<span style=\"color:#222222\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">\u266b</span></span>","edited":{"userid":7593,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","user":"Teatime","date":". 01-13-2016, 01:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7593-teatime","name":"Teatime","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7593_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"750"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T10:13:23-08:00","id":807990,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mlvalentine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807976#post807976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm going to ask you a ridiculously challenging question then. With what you posted in mind: What's the difference between a serial killer and a <i>hunter</i>?</div> </div> </div> </div>A hunter has a problem with killing. He'll do it if he has to; but like a soldier or a police officer who kills someone, killing is still a Breaking Point for him. For a serial killer, there's no problem.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T10:14:52-08:00","id":807991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mlvalentine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807976#post807976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm going to ask you a ridiculously challenging question then. With what you posted in mind: What's the difference between a serial killer and a <i>hunter</i>?</div> </div> </div> </div>My I answer this also?<br /> <br /> For me - Hunter <i>does not need to kill</i>. Once again, we have canonical groups that do not kill monsters - at least as base Modus Operandi. <i>Null Mysteriis</i>, <i>Network Zero</i>, <i>Loyalists of Thule</i>, <i>Les Mysteres</i>, <i>VASCU</i> - they do not kill others - they &quot;solve cases&quot;, And still they are all treated as Hunters. Because Vigil is about obssession on <b><i>confronting</i></b> monsters, not killing all around.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T10:21:47-08:00","id":807998,"title":"","content":"I like where this is going. <br /> <br /> Another pertinent question: do Hunters retire, and can Slashers overcome their need to kill? I distinctly remember reading a side bar in the second draft of Beast that said even Heroes can leave their self-centered quest behind, rare as that may be. Why wouldn't Slashers also be capable? Admittedly, a lot are probably too broken or simply evil to expect that from them, and Scourges are even further removed from humanity. But it seems like something that players an STs might ask themselves, especially if the Slasher used to be one of the PCs' greatest friends. That's a gold mine of conflict and drama, right there. You just need a serious and empathic outlook on the story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":4443,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","user":"GibberingEloquence","date":". 01-13-2016, 01:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T10:25:56-08:00","id":808005,"title":"","content":"Could we get a Compact of therapists dedicated to rehabilitating Slashers?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T10:34:03-08:00","id":808011,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure this needs a Compact myself. IMO Rippers can be arrested by normal cops, and cured by normal therapists. They're not actually supernatural. Scourges on the other hand are too far gone, you can't cure a Vampire, or rehabiliate a Scourge.<br /> <br /> In fact. That's pat of what makes Rippers so interesting to me. Take your typical Union Cell. They're a band of armed vigilanties. They're also quite possibly the best qualified people to deal with a borderline supernatural threat like a Ripper. But Rippers are only borderline, so ethically don't they deserve due process? Should The Union leave it to the cops?<br /> <br /> <br /> Hmmm. Speaking of leaving Rippers to the cops. Perhaps your &quot;therapist Compact&quot; is actually VASCU <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mlvalentine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807976#post807976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm going to ask you a ridiculously challenging question then. With what you posted in mind: What's the difference between a serial killer and a <i>hunter</i>?</div> </div> </div> </div>My answer - a serial killer is a legal term. An executioner or soldier employed by the state is not a serial killer.<br /> <br /> A hunter who stakes multiple vampires might be depending on the personhood of vampire; and wheather the judge buys the &quot;killing vampires is self-defence by default&quot; argument.<br /> <br /> A Network Zero hunter who never kills anyone is definately not a serial killer.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T10:38:28-08:00","id":808018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GottaGoFeast</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807998#post807998\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another pertinent question: do Hunters retire, and can Slashers overcome their need to kill? I distinctly remember reading a side bar in the second draft of Beast that said even Heroes can leave their self-centered quest behind, rare as that may be. Why wouldn't Slashers also be capable? Admittedly, a lot are probably too broken or simply evil to expect that from them, and Scourges are even further removed from humanity. But it seems like something that players an STs might ask themselves, especially if the Slasher used to be one of the PCs' greatest friends. That's a gold mine of conflict and drama, right there. You just need a serious and empathic outlook on the story.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would let truly abstaining Slasher to change his Aspiration from killing someone - or, better, to <b>not let</b> him change. Slasherdom, in mine point of view, is <i>compulsion</i>, is <i>urge</i>. Is <i>instinct</i>. You can fight it all the time - <b>Dexter</b> in his series so much tries with that - but it <i>will</i> hover over all your life. Limiting Aspiration pool constantly by locking one you<i> do not </i>fulfill sounds as good showing of that - you can resist all the way you want, maybe even spending years of abstaining. But in truth you still just want to slit throats all along...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T11:07:51-08:00","id":808056,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807920#post807920\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">DaveB has mentioned in the past that humans in CofD have a tendency to shy away from the supernatural. That is why one of the five Breaking Point questions in the CofD Rulebook is \u201cwhat [supernatural encounter] have you forgotten?\u201d \u2014 the premise behind that question is that virtually everyone in the setting has had an encounter with the supernatural at one point or another, and nearly all of them have put that encounter out of their mind, willfully forgetting what they saw. The protagonists of the CofD core book are among these \u201cordinary people\u201d, but have recently had (or are about to have) an encounter with the darkness that they cannot ignore. That's the framework of the \u201cdefault\u201d Chronicle of Darkness: ordinary people dealing as best they can with the horrors and nightmares that lurk in the shadows, despite their natural inclination to hide from it and pretend it doesn't exist.<br /> <br /> By contrast, a hunter is a human who willfully confronts the monsters on a regular basis. Whatever it is that causes ordinary people to ignore the monsters has no hold on the hunter; instead, he or she does the exact opposite and <i>seeks out</i> the supernatural. That's what the Vigil is: a reversal of humanity's normal instincts concerning the CofD's mysteries.<br /> <br /> That's the conceptual distinction between a \u201cblue book mortal\u201d and a hunter, as I see it. The mechanical distinction is almost entirely embodied in Practical Experience and its applications (in 1e, Risking Willpower is the only \u201ctemplate ability\u201d that hunters have that isn't purchased with Practical Experience): as I like to think of it, Practical Experience is what a hunter learns from an encounter that a non-hunter would overlook or forget.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's my preferred answer as well. A person becomes a hunter and takes up the Vigil when the scales are ripped from their eyes and the darkness is laid bare. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mlvalentine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807976#post807976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I'm going to ask you a ridiculously challenging question then. With what you posted in mind: What's the difference between a serial killer and a <i>hunter</i>?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure if this is an either/or question. To me, the blurry nature of the distinction between a hunter and a serial killer or slasher is part of the point. Hunters walk a razor-thin line. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/186-morty","name":"Morty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar186_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T11:22:27-08:00","id":808071,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SpanishInquisition</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807961#post807961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">[SIZE=14px]<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I completely agree with this. In fact in my own GMC / Mortal Remains Hunter the Vigil game I've replaced the question, &amp;quot;What have you forgotten?&amp;quot; with &amp;quot;What can't you forget?&amp;quot; Which served to give players a definite motivation to hunt and me ideas about what sort of monsters they want to see. For example one PC survived a Slasher attack, when the Cell encountered a Slasher it put his character in the spotlight. Hunters are human - they're just incredibly committed they can't turn back.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>I know this isn't a popularity contest, but simply Liking this post doesn't do it justice. As well, it doesn't let me specify exactly what I like: replacing \u201cwhat have you forgotten?\u201d with \u201cwhat can't you forget?\u201d is brilliant!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T12:47:53-08:00","id":808146,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=808056#post808056\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure if this is an either/or question. To me, the blurry nature of the distinction between a hunter and a serial killer or slasher is part of the point. Hunters walk a razor-thin line.</div> </div> </div> </div> For what it's worth, blurry distinctions are great \u2014 in the game. On the character sheet, some more precision is called for. But yeah; one reason I suggested counting a serial killer as a \u201cfirst-stage Slasher\u201d was to blur the line.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T12:52:15-08:00","id":808148,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807990#post807990\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A hunter has a problem with killing. He'll do it if he has to; but like a soldier or a police officer who kills someone, killing is still a Breaking Point for him. For a serial killer, there's no problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know if that's necessarily true. Like I said in my comment on the devpost, a hunter's reluctance to kill is largely contextual and from my experience tied to how closely they can relate to the monster they're hunting. A very inhuman and unnatural creature seems easier to just kill remorselessly, the same way you or I might smash a mosquito but on a larger scale. Or go a step further and think about actual 'hunters' in the mundane world who cull dangerous invasive species. They most likely have no problem with their killing. The rub comes when, as a hunter, you start extending that attitude to increasingly broader definitions of 'monster'. The serial killer starts from the zero-mark, often seeing human life as having no more value than an insect or small animal; it exists solely to be used and destroyed for their satisfaction.<br /> <br /> EDIT: To put a more mechanical spin on this; would you require a Breaking Point for a Hunter killing a particularly inhuman manifested Angel? Putting aside any Breaking Points for simply encountering such a creature, is there (or should there be) personal degeneration for what is essentially turning off a biomechanical automaton?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Will Rotenberry</b><br /> Onyx Path Freelancer<br /> <b>Armory: Hurt Locker</b>","edited":{"userid":5031,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5031-bloodywillpower","user":"BloodyWillpower","date":".  tried to clarify my post by posing a related question 01-13-2016, 03:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5031-bloodywillpower","name":"BloodyWillpower","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5031_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T12:58:49-08:00","id":808152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=807991#post807991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> My I answer this also?<br /> <br /> For me - Hunter <i>does not need to kill</i>. Once again, we have canonical groups that do not kill monsters - at least as base Modus Operandi. <i>Null Mysteriis</i>, <i>Network Zero</i>, <i>Loyalists of Thule</i>, <i>Les Mysteres</i>, <i>VASCU</i> - they do not kill others - they &amp;quot;solve cases&amp;quot;, And still they are all treated as Hunters. Because Vigil is about obssession on <b><i>confronting</i></b> monsters, not killing all around.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with this 100%. There are tons of Hunters that will work with the supernatural if there is greater gain. There are pleanty that will kill, but there are many that don't have that as part of their MO and will only do so in self defence. Before I backed Doubting Souls, this was one of my hurdles with Hunter. Once I realized they didn't have to fill, I fell in love with the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><a href=\"https://tinyurl.com/2p8ss75f\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Chris H</b></a>/<b>EndDaysEngine</b> | <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/EndDaysEngine\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Patreon</b></a></span>| <b>He/His </b>| <b>Currently Writing</b>: <i>[Redacted], [Redacted]</i><br /> <br /> <b>CofD booklists: </b><a href=\"https://goo.gl/DRdB7h\" target=\"_blank\">Beast</a> I <a href=\"https://goo.gl/x1FPpM\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/ZSCx5U\" target=\"_blank\">Demon</a> | <a href=\"http://shorturl.at/syDK9\" target=\"_blank\">Deviant</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/Mh5M6m\" target=\"_blank\">Geist</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/gSjfm5\" target=\"_blank\">Hunter</a> l <a href=\"https://goo.gl/qj5aX9\" target=\"_blank\">Mage</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/2HCpjg\" target=\"_blank\">Mummy</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/68yRHY\" target=\"_blank\">Promethean</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/a4mjqA\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> | <a href=\"https://goo.gl/hrULtN\" target=\"_blank\">Werewolf</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4433-second-chances","name":"Second Chances","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T13:29:12-08:00","id":808180,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=808146#post808146\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> For what it's worth, blurry distinctions are great \u2014 in the game. On the character sheet, some more precision is called for. But yeah; one reason I suggested counting a serial killer as a \u201cfirst-stage Slasher\u201d was to blur the line.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is true. I was talking about blurriness from an in-character and in-story perspective. But it's a different discussion in terms of game concepts and mechanics.<br /> <br /> In some ways, the difference between a &quot;normal&quot; serial killer and a slasher isn't unlike that between an investigator played using the core CoD book and a hunter. There are many people who kill others, for some reason. But a slasher is removed from normalcy and sanity by another step.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":186,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/186-morty","user":"Morty","date":". 01-13-2016, 04:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/186-morty","name":"Morty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar186_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T15:00:29-08:00","id":808258,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=808180#post808180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That is true. I was talking about blurriness from an in-character and in-story perspective. But it's a different discussion in terms of game concepts and mechanics.<br /> <br /> In some ways, the difference between a &quot;normal&quot; serial killer and a slasher isn't unlike that between an investigator played using the core CoD book and a hunter. There are many people who kill others, for some reason. But a slasher is removed from normalcy and sanity by another step.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's precisely my point, by the way. When I think about these concepts, I have to envision them holistically to see how their definition in the setting might be enhanced and supported by the rules. So, for my purposes, a serial killer and a Hunter wouldn't necessarily be the same. But, I dig the idea of serial killer as being on the path to becoming a Slasher in some fashion.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1870-mlvalentine","name":"mlvalentine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1870_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T17:22:26-08:00","id":808327,"title":"","content":"Been reading over the thread and here are my observations.<br /> <br /> <b>Civilian v. Hunter</b><br /> <br /> Setting aside the mechanical considerations of Blue Book v. HtV, I like the idea that a Hunter for the most part is an initially normal with a degree more drive/obsession. The access to Endowments /Supernatural Merits isn't really what make a Hunter, nor are game concepts like Risking Willpower or the like. The big difference becomes the question stated earlier - &quot;What cannot be Forgotten&quot;, which I would follow with &quot;What are You going to do about it?&quot;.<br /> A character that lives in a haunted house will ignore and rationalize the strangeness until it does something that can no longer be ignored (like possessing your 8 year old). After much research and a harrowing exorcism, the house is cleansed. Most people would move far away, and put the whole ugliness behind them, eventual no longer believing even their own memories about the events. A Hunter, or someone on the path to becoming one, won't let it go. They will continue to collect stories and bookmark web articles. One day, they will hear about someone else with a possessed child, and then they will go and <i>Do Something About It</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>Serial Killer v. Slasher</b><br /> The world is full of killers, but not every killer is a Slasher. In the vast majority of cases, there is a reason or justification for taking a life (casualty of war, duty, greed, compulsion), but even in the case of sadistic murders (the sort of people who DO have murderous Aspirations) , I don't think this is enough to make the killer anything more than a twisted but regretfully mundane human.<br /> A Slasher is <i>wrong</i> on a fundamental level. They suffer some sort of trauma so deep or obsession so profound that it damages their very Souls. And then something dark fills in the cracks. Perhaps they are an alternate consequence of Soul Loss. Either way once the cracks grow large enough, dark powers and strange capabilities begin to manifest, creating a True Scourge.<br /> You can have a serial killer who just sliced up their 5th cheerleader. They are cornered by the cops and shot several times, falling into the water. For a &quot;normal&quot; killer, that would be the end of it; the cops pull the body out of the water and that is that. But for others this moment crosses a threshold. The Killer pulls himself out of the water 30 minutes later, their wounds less severe than they had seemed. The Killer asks themselves how they survived, and they already knows the answer &quot; Because they Aren't Done&quot;.<br /> <br /> --Or thats at least how I see it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1420009-the-artificer-s-workshop\" target=\"_blank\">The Artificer's Workshop</a> - A collection of Exalted Artifacts<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/806809-curios-relics-and-tomes\" target=\"_blank\">Curios, Relics, and Tomes</a> - A collection of Relics (Cursed and Otherwise)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/793173-the-horror-show\" target=\"_blank\">The Horror Lab</a> - A collection of Beasts, Monsters and less definable things.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1128197-strange-places#post1128197\" target=\"_blank\">Strange Places</a> - A collection of Dark, Mysterious, and Wondrous Locations<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/993181-twilight-menagerie\" target=\"_blank\">Twilight Menagerie</a> - A collection of Ephemeral Entities","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/775-reighnhell","name":"Reighnhell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar775_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"749"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T17:30:56-08:00","id":808331,"title":"","content":"Just curious, why the author's strong opposition to making Slasher's something supernatural (w/ dread powers and such)?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5685-archivist","name":"Archivist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T17:39:42-08:00","id":808334,"title":"","content":"I think the idea is to blur the line between Slashers and Hunters.<br /> <br /> I think overtly supernatural Slashers, with Dread Powers and the like, can serve as a good example of what happens when a Slasher is allowed to thrive. Much like a weed, if they're not removed when they are still in the &quot;blurred lines&quot; stage, they tap into all the worst vices and cruelties of mankind and drink deep from the well of humanity's atrocities.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-13T19:16:33-08:00","id":808379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Reighnhell</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=808327#post808327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Been reading over the thread and here are my observations.<br /> <br /> <b>Civilian v. Hunter</b><br /> <br /> Setting aside the mechanical considerations of Blue Book v. HtV, I like the idea that a Hunter for the most part is an initially normal with a degree more drive/obsession. The access to Endowments /Supernatural Merits isn't really what make a Hunter, nor are game concepts like Risking Willpower or the like. The big difference becomes the question stated earlier - &amp;quot;What cannot be Forgotten&amp;quot;, which I would follow with &amp;quot;What are You going to do about it?&amp;quot;.<br /> A character that lives in a haunted house will ignore and rationalize the strangeness until it does something that can no longer be ignored (like possessing your 8 year old). After much research and a harrowing exorcism, the house is cleansed. Most people would move far away, and put the whole ugliness behind them, eventual no longer believing even their own memories about the events. A Hunter, or someone on the path to becoming one, won't let it go. They will continue to collect stories and bookmark web articles. One day, they will hear about someone else with a possessed child, and then they will go and <i>Do Something About It</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>FWIW, \u201cwhat are you going to do about it?\u201d is where Aspirations come in, as opposed to the five Breaking Point questions. Certainly, Hunter character creation should pose both of these questions. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Reighnhell</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=808327#post808327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Serial Killer v. Slasher</b><br /> The world is full of killers, but not every killer is a Slasher. In the vast majority of cases, there is a reason or justification for taking a life (casualty of war, duty, greed, compulsion), but even in the case of sadistic murders (the sort of people who DO have murderous Aspirations) , I don't think this is enough to make the killer anything more than a twisted but regretfully mundane human.<br /> A Slasher is <i>wrong</i> on a fundamental level. They suffer some sort of trauma so deep or obsession so profound that it damages their very Souls. And then something dark fills in the cracks. Perhaps they are an alternate consequence of Soul Loss. Either way once the cracks grow large enough, dark powers and strange capabilities begin to manifest, creating a True Scourge.<br /> You can have a serial killer who just sliced up their 5th cheerleader. They are cornered by the cops and shot several times, falling into the water. For a &amp;quot;normal&amp;quot; killer, that would be the end of it; the cops pull the body out of the water and that is that. But for others this moment crosses a threshold. The Killer pulls himself out of the water 30 minutes later, their wounds less severe than they had seemed. The Killer asks themselves how they survived, and they already knows the answer &amp;quot; Because they Aren't Done&amp;quot;.<br /> <br /> --Or thats at least how I see it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much, yeah \u2014 at least, that's the case for Scourges. I see Killers, Rippers, and Scourges thematically echoing the three-tier structure of Hunters, where Killers are otherwise-ordinary people who have no inhibitions about killing (much like Tier One hunters are otherwise-ordinary people who confront the supernatural instead of hiding from it); Rippers are Killers who have been empowered to kill more effectively but not in a way that's definitely supernatural (much like Tier Two hunters are empowered to confront the supernatural without becoming supernatural); and Scourges are what you get when the Slasher becomes a supernatural monster (much like Tier Three hunters \u201cfight fire with fire\u201d, bringing supernatural Endowments to bear against supernatural monsters while arguably becoming supernatural themselves).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T01:14:33-08:00","id":808509,"title":"","content":"<b>Hunter:</b> For the me &quot;ur-est of the ur-&quot; nonsupernatural hunter is exemplified by the Union, and by most tier 1 cells. They find something supernatural menacing their vicinity and know that someone has to deal with it however and with whatever they can. Which isn't unlike blue book mortals, but the hunter is defined by the fact that they don't look away once they've seen the dark that first time.<br /> <br /> <i>Heroic:</i> A heroic hunter in this vein is motivated primarily by saving the monsters' present and future victims (and will probably expand the number of people they feel responsible for protecting over time) - certainly beyond just them and theirs. Equally, if there <i>aren't</i> any victims, why does a monster deserve to die.<br /> <br /> <i>Villainous:</i> The first step to a villainous hunter is being motivated primarily by killing monster just because it's a wrong thing, rather than because it's hurting someone. And by seeing other people as tools in this hunt.<br /> <br /> [b]Slasher:[/i] I'd see a slasher analogous to this type of hunter as someone driven to hunt a type of human rather than a type of monster.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T02:41:51-08:00","id":808543,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=808379#post808379\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pretty much, yeah \u2014 at least, that's the case for Scourges. I see Killers, Rippers, and Scourges thematically echoing the three-tier structure of Hunters, where Killers are otherwise-ordinary people who have no inhibitions about killing (much like Tier One hunters are otherwise-ordinary people who confront the supernatural instead of hiding from it); Rippers are Killers who have been empowered to kill more effectively but not in a way that's definitely supernatural (much like Tier Two hunters are empowered to confront the supernatural without becoming supernatural); and Scourges are what you get when the Slasher becomes a supernatural monster (much like Tier Three hunters \u201cfight fire with fire\u201d, bringing supernatural Endowments to bear against supernatural monsters while arguably becoming supernatural themselves).</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with this points - I only made Rippers as more &quot;Killers with Tactics and some special Merits&quot;. Scourges are clearly supernatural, Rippers are &quot;only&quot; experienced or driven much more than normal Killers. If Jason Voorhes ( <i>Friday 13th</i> movies ) and Micheal Mayeres ( <i>Halloween</i> movies ), both Freddie Kruger and Candymen are both <i>doubly</i> supernatural Scourages ( being goetia and ghosts Scorages, respectivly ). Then Jigsaw ( <i>Saw</i> movies), Ghostface ( <i>Scream</i> movies ), Hannibal Lecter (<i>Silence of Lambs </i>movies and TV series) and Dexter ( his TV series ) are Rippers. Then &quot;only&quot; Killers are characters like <a href=\"http://dexter.wikia.com/wiki/Lumen_Pierce\" target=\"_blank\">Lumen Pierce</a> or <a href=\"http://dexter.wikia.com/wiki/Hannah_McKay\" target=\"_blank\">Hannah McKay</a> - they killed few people, but those deaths were more coincidences than total drive on the characters. Also, Dexter has mentoring them both, with his experience, like Tier 2 Hunter character the Tier 1 newbies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T08:02:28-08:00","id":808705,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Archivist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=808331#post808331\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just curious, why the author's strong opposition to making Slasher's something supernatural (w/ dread powers and such)?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not strongly opposed to Slashers using Dread Powers, and please remember that this is in brainstorming phase. In many cases, if a hunter becomes a slasher, I don't think it makes sense for that character to automatically gain the benefit of Dread Powers. I'm suggesting that there should be a progression, but that is also impacted by what type of hunter you are and how much you interact with the supernatural.<br /> <br /> Also, I'd appreciate it if you'd use my name. Thank you! <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1870-mlvalentine","name":"mlvalentine","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1870_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"140"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T20:10:03-08:00","id":809318,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">I've always viewed Hunters and Slashers as distinct but related phenomena. Without getting too granular, a Hunter is someone who has been exposed to the supernatural and has chosen to act against it in some way. It is tempting to say that they \u201cfight\u201d it, but groups like Network Zero, Null Mysteriis, the Barrett Commission, and Habibti Ma take an approach that is often non-violent while still thwarting the supernatural. This also distinguishes Hunters from what I call Thralls and Bystanders. Thralls in this case are any non-supernatural individuals who have been exposed to the supernatural but have chosen instead to ally themselves with it. Bystanders by the same token are those who have been exposed but choose not to get involved, either by looking the other way or by not taking an active role in the Vigil.<br /> <br /> Slashers to me are slightly different animals depending on whether we are talking about Rippers or Scourges. Rippers are mundane serial killers, if such a term can be applied to them. They are monstrous by dint of their actions but are ultimately mere mortals and a very real sort of horror. Scourges on the other hand are a product of folklore. They exist to ape the tropes of serial killers as they exist in horror fiction and function as monsters in the same vein as other supernaturals.<br /> <br /> Where things get interesting is in the commonalities between Hunters and Slashers. Hunters find it necessary to be violent because of the exigencies of the Vigil. Slashers find it necessary to be violent in order to satisfy a compulsion. Hunters kill things which are not \u201chuman\u201d (depending on your definition) but still cause the deaths of sentient beings. That is going to cause a reasonable person some amount of mental and emotional stress, and the very mindset that dehumanizes supernatural \u201cmonsters\u201d can work against a well meaning Hunter trying to hold on to his Integrity. To me Hunters and Slashers are not two sides of a coin but rather two colors of dye. You can add red dye to blue and while they may mingle into something that is not blue you can still say that it is not red. Up to a point. If you keep adding dye though it is going to get harder to call it anything but red. I tend to imagine this happening usually one of two ways. The first is mission bleed. You start by only killing monsters, then you start killing their minions and thralls, then you start killing people who are associated with them or corrupted by them, and then where does it end? The other is Maslow's hammer, i. e. \u201cwhen all you have is a hammer everything starts to look like a nail.\u201d Those Hunters who have grown accustomed to using violence in their Vigil may find that their comfort with violence has other advantages. Intimidating people to get what they need, using collateral damage to discourage retaliation from the supernatural, or even simply killing people who make themselves obstacles to the Vigil. Left unchecked these Hunters may find themselves unable to function in ways that aren't violent. In both cases, Nietzche should always be in mind. \u201c<span style=\"color:#252525\">He who fights with monsters should look to it that he himself does not become a monster.\u201d </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252525\">Games that I have played in have toyed with the idea of Hunters becoming Slashers. Mostly this has occurred in an NPC context, but PCs have also flirted with it through things like the Cortical Adaptation and the Mask of Terror. The results have been mixed, but I would say that the table experiences were rewarding nonetheless and this is something I would like to see explored further in 2E.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252525\">I am ambivalent as to how I feel about Slasher cabals. While I like the idea behind groups like the Hunt Club and the Subtle Collectors Association, I've always tended to view Slashers as rather self-centered and solitary. I also think that having groups of Slashers would diminish their \u201cspecial-ness\u201d somewhat, while also running the risk of coming off hokey if their organization is too widespread or well-entrenched that it stretches credulity. That being said I can already think of several examples of Slasher cabals that I'd want to use in a game, so the idea is not without some story potential. I can envision a clan of religious fanatics who ritualistically select a sinner and devour their debased worldly flesh so that their soul might ascend to heaven. I can see a shady travel agency that does \u201cmurder tourism\u201d</span><br /> <span style=\"color:#252525\">for the discerning psychopath. I can picture a cult of teenagers that worship an infamous Slasher and seek to recreate their crimes in meticulous detail.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252525\">I am also a fan of using the Conditions system to represent/facilitate a Hunter's slide into Slasher-hood. I feel this works even better than simply using Morality and Derangements, as the latter implies that Slashers are what all Hunters will become if they degenerate, while the former makes the transformation somewhat more deliberate while still being organic. </span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2507,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","user":"Caitiff Primogen","date":". 01-14-2016, 11:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T20:27:18-08:00","id":809331,"title":"","content":"The discussion of Dread Powers is rather interesting. For one thing, I love Dread Powers. Their very modular setup is rife with opportunities for kitbashing and customization, and despite how general they are I still find them excellent for mining plot hooks. I can understand the idea that Hunters should not be using them. Dread Powers are in most cases explicitly supernatural and fly in the face of Hunters being normal mortals. That being said I feel that once one has started using Endowments the line into the supernatural has been effectively crossed, which is a thematic conflict I greatly enjoy in Hunter. To put this in a Slasher context, a Ripper seems like an inappropriate choice for Dread Powers. They are at their essence a &quot;normal&quot; mortal just like a Hunter, and their Undertaking advantage reflects this. Scourges on the other hand have taken two lumbering implacable steps into the realm of the supernatural. They are elemental, and are given the ability to perform impossible feats of terror. With this in mind I would be a fan of allowing Scourges some limited access to Dread Powers in addition to their Undertaking advantage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T20:56:28-08:00","id":809350,"title":"","content":"<b>Cabals</b><br /> <br /> I rather like the option of an associations of Slashers.<br /> Genre wise, there is precedence - Two Thousand Maniacs (1964), Last House on the Left (1972), Texas Chainsaw Massacre (1974), The Hills Have Eyes (1977), House of 1000 Corpses/Devils Rejects (2003/2005), Hostel (2005) all feature groups of Killers rather than the archetypal lone stalker.<br /> <br /> You can even cite the crimes of the &quot;Manson Family&quot; as a real world example of an organized group of killers.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1420009-the-artificer-s-workshop\" target=\"_blank\">The Artificer's Workshop</a> - A collection of Exalted Artifacts<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/806809-curios-relics-and-tomes\" target=\"_blank\">Curios, Relics, and Tomes</a> - A collection of Relics (Cursed and Otherwise)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/793173-the-horror-show\" target=\"_blank\">The Horror Lab</a> - A collection of Beasts, Monsters and less definable things.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1128197-strange-places#post1128197\" target=\"_blank\">Strange Places</a> - A collection of Dark, Mysterious, and Wondrous Locations<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/993181-twilight-menagerie\" target=\"_blank\">Twilight Menagerie</a> - A collection of Ephemeral Entities","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/775-reighnhell","name":"Reighnhell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar775_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"749"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T21:25:39-08:00","id":809361,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=809331#post809331\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The discussion of Dread Powers is rather interesting. For one thing, I love Dread Powers. Their very modular setup is rife with opportunities for kitbashing and customization, and despite how general they are I still find them excellent for mining plot hooks. I can understand the idea that Hunters should not be using them. Dread Powers are in most cases explicitly supernatural and fly in the face of Hunters being normal mortals. That being said I feel that once one has started using Endowments the line into the supernatural has been effectively crossed, which is a thematic conflict I greatly enjoy in Hunter. To put this in a Slasher context, a Ripper seems like an inappropriate choice for Dread Powers. They are at their essence a &amp;quot;normal&amp;quot; mortal just like a Hunter, and their Undertaking advantage reflects this. Scourges on the other hand have taken two lumbering implacable steps into the realm of the supernatural. They are elemental, and are given the ability to perform impossible feats of terror. With this in mind I would be a fan of allowing Scourges some limited access to Dread Powers in addition to their Undertaking advantage.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is pretty much what I was thinking. If 2e still has something more or less like 1e's Undertakings, I think it would be appropriate for each Scourge's Undertaking to list two or three sample Dread Powers that the Scourge might end up with. <br /> <br /> Heck, if it weren't for the fact that the downside of a Scourge's Undertaking was different (in degree, kind, or both) from its Ripper Undertaking counterpart, I'd suggest ditching the Scourge Undertakings entirely and operating everything off of 1e's Ripper Undertakings, converting the powers granted by 1e's Scourge Undertakings into Undertaking-specific \u201csignature Dread Powers\u201d that are unlocked and acquired as part of becoming a Scourge, with other Dread Powers possibly to follow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-14T23:41:33-08:00","id":809397,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mlvalentine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=808705#post808705\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not strongly opposed to Slashers using Dread Powers, and please remember that this is in brainstorming phase. In many cases, if a hunter becomes a slasher, I don't think it makes sense for that character to automatically gain the benefit of Dread Powers. I'm suggesting that there should be a progression, but that is also impacted by what type of hunter you are and how much you interact with the supernatural.<br /> <br /> Also, I'd appreciate it if you'd use my name. Thank you!</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally I wouldn't mind if Slashers use Dread Powers, but I would very much like to keep the current set up of each Undertaking having an advantage and disadvantage, and gaining a second of each at scourges. IMO the Undertakings are the most interesting part of Slashers and I think their signature powers are what makes them work.<br /> <br /> However if, for example, a Mask's nigh-invulnerability was a Dread Power that you might also see other monsters have. I'd be fine with that.<br /> <br /> I'd also be fine if Scourges had the ability to advance further and gain additional abilities. To use a famous slasher example - Jason is definitely a Mask and he has the indestructibility down. He also comes back from the dead repeatedly, including explicitly escaping hell in Freddy vs Jason. So I imagine the way to represent him mechanically is that when he became a Mask he got the signature invulnerability, then he advanced further and unlocked resurrection.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":134,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","user":"The Kings Raven","date":". 01-15-2016, 02:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-01-15T09:06:26-08:00","id":809628,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mlvalentine</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/807844-hunter-2e-open-dev-follow-up-hunters-and-slashers?p=808705#post808705\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not strongly opposed to Slashers using Dread Powers, and please remember that this is in brainstorming phase. In many cases, if a hunter becomes a slasher, I don't think it makes sense for that character to automatically gain the benefit of Dread Powers. I'm suggesting that there should be a progression, but that is also impacted by what type of hunter you are and how much you interact with the supernatural.<br /> <br /> Also, I'd appreciate it if you'd use my name. Thank you! </div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry I didn't remember who the author was or what the post was","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5685-archivist","name":"Archivist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5685_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"363"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}